-- ============================================================
-- ACT Reading – 120 Questions (3 Practice Papers)
-- 4 passages per paper × 10 questions per passage = 40 q/paper
-- Passages stored as question_groups (contexttext field)
--
-- Assumes seed_act.sql already ran:
--   Course "ACT Reading", units queue=1 (Literary Narrative)
--   and queue=2 (Social Science / Humanities / Natural Science)
--   already exist. Paper 1 (10 q) already exists.
--
-- Creates: Reading Practice Test 2, 3, 4 (40 q each, 35 min)
-- ============================================================

DO $$
DECLARE
    v_read_id    INTEGER;
    v_read_unit1 INTEGER;   -- Literary Narrative & Prose Fiction
    v_read_unit2 INTEGER;   -- Social Science, Humanities & Natural Science
    v_read_p2    INTEGER;
    v_read_p3    INTEGER;
    v_read_p4    INTEGER;
    v_group_id   INTEGER;
BEGIN

    -- ── Resolve existing course & units ───────────────────────
    SELECT idcourse INTO v_read_id
    FROM courses WHERE lower(coursename) = 'act reading' LIMIT 1;
    IF v_read_id IS NULL THEN RAISE EXCEPTION 'Course "ACT Reading" not found.'; END IF;

    SELECT idunit INTO v_read_unit1
    FROM units WHERE idcourse = v_read_id AND queue = 1 LIMIT 1;
    SELECT idunit INTO v_read_unit2
    FROM units WHERE idcourse = v_read_id AND queue = 2 LIMIT 1;
    IF v_read_unit1 IS NULL THEN RAISE EXCEPTION 'Unit 1 (Literary Narrative) not found.'; END IF;
    IF v_read_unit2 IS NULL THEN RAISE EXCEPTION 'Unit 2 (Nonfiction) not found.'; END IF;

    -- ── Papers ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
    VALUES (v_read_id, 'Reading Practice Test 2',
        'This test contains four passages, each followed by ten questions. Choose the best answer based on what is stated or implied in the passage.',
        'MCQ', 40, 35, 2)
    RETURNING idpaper INTO v_read_p2;

    VALUES (v_read_id, 'Reading Practice Test 3',
        'This test contains four passages, each followed by ten questions. Choose the best answer based on what is stated or implied in the passage.',
        'MCQ', 40, 35, 3)
    RETURNING idpaper INTO v_read_p3;

    VALUES (v_read_id, 'Reading Practice Test 4',
        'This test contains four passages, each followed by ten questions. Choose the best answer based on what is stated or implied in the passage.',
        'MCQ', 40, 35, 4)
    RETURNING idpaper INTO v_read_p4;

    -- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
    -- PAPER 2 – READING PRACTICE TEST 2
    -- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

    -- ── PASSAGE 1 [Literary Narrative]: "The Music Box" ────────
    VALUES (v_read_p2, 'Literary Narrative: "The Music Box"',
'LITERARY NARRATIVE: Adapted from a short story (© 2019).

The drive from Portland took six hours, long enough for Clara to rehearse everything she intended to say to the house — the apologies, the recriminations, the explanations she had never managed to deliver while her mother was alive. By the time she turned onto the county road, she had run out of words entirely.

The house was smaller than she remembered. Memory inflates everything it touches: the rooms had seemed cathedral-high when she was eight, the backyard a wilderness. Now the porch sagged at one corner, and the rosebushes her mother had tended with what Clara once considered obsession had grown wild and unpruned past the fence.

She found the music box on the third day, in the bottom drawer of the bureau, beneath folded sweaters that still held the faint cedar smell of the wooden blocks her mother had placed in every drawer. It was a simple thing — lacquered black, with a tiny silver clasp. Clara had never seen it before.

The melody it played was a waltz, slightly off-key from age. Clara sat on the edge of the stripped mattress and listened to it repeat — once, twice, three times — before she finally let herself cry.

In the weeks that followed, she would learn that the music box had been a gift from a man whose name Clara''s grandmother had refused to speak for forty years. That information would change nothing and everything. But in that moment, alone in the afternoon light, Clara held the small object and understood only that her mother had kept something beautiful hidden, and that some part of that hiding had been grief.',
    1) RETURNING id INTO v_group_id;


    -- ── PASSAGE 2 [Social Science]: "The Bystander Effect" ─────
    VALUES (v_read_p2, 'Social Science: "The Bystander Effect"',
'SOCIAL SCIENCE: Adapted from "The Psychology of Inaction" (© 2021).

In March 1964, Kitty Genovese was murdered outside her apartment building in New York City, and newspaper reports — later found to be exaggerated — claimed that dozens of neighbors heard her cries and did nothing. Whether or not the details were accurate, the case prompted psychologists John Darley and Bibb Latané to design experiments investigating why people fail to help in emergencies when others are present.

In their landmark 1968 study, participants believed they were listening to another student having a seizure over an intercom. When participants believed they were the sole witness, 85 percent sought help within sixty seconds. When they believed five other people were also listening, only 31 percent intervened, and their response times were significantly longer.

Darley and Latané identified two mechanisms. The first, diffusion of responsibility, holds that in a crowd each individual feels less personally obligated to act because others could share the burden. The second, pluralistic ignorance, describes a situation in which each bystander looks to others for cues about whether to act — and because everyone appears calm, everyone assumes the situation is not serious.

More recent research has complicated this picture. A 2019 meta-analysis reviewing nearly 7,500 real-world incidents captured on CCTV in three countries found that at least one bystander intervened in 90 percent of cases. The researchers argued that real emergencies — involving direct eye contact, visible distress, and physical proximity — may activate helping behaviors that artificial laboratory conditions suppress.',
    2) RETURNING id INTO v_group_id;


    -- ── PASSAGE 3 [Humanities]: "The Birth of Jazz" ────────────
    VALUES (v_read_p2, 'Humanities: "The Birth of Jazz"',
'HUMANITIES: Adapted from "New Orleans and the American Sound" (© 2020).

When jazz emerged from New Orleans in the first decades of the twentieth century, it was less an invention than a convergence. African rhythmic complexity — syncopation, polyrhythm, call-and-response — fused with European harmonic structures, the emotional directness of Delta blues, and the formal precision of ragtime. The result was a music whose defining characteristic was improvisation: not the abandonment of structure but an ongoing negotiation between structure and spontaneity.

New Orleans was uniquely equipped for this synthesis. A port city with a history of French and Spanish colonial rule, it had developed a creole culture that blended African, Caribbean, and European influences with unusual openness. Congo Square, where enslaved people gathered on Sundays to maintain African musical traditions through drumming and dance, preserved the rhythmic foundations that would eventually flow into jazz.

Cornetist Buddy Bolden is often identified as the first jazz musician, though his career is difficult to document — no recordings survive, and knowledge of him comes almost entirely from the recollections of musicians who heard him play around the turn of the twentieth century. What those musicians consistently described was a player of extraordinary power: according to legend, he could be heard from miles away on a still night.

By the time Louis Armstrong reached New York in the 1920s, jazz had traveled north with the Great Migration, which carried hundreds of thousands of African Americans — and their music — to Chicago, Detroit, and Harlem, where it helped ignite one of the most vibrant cultural explosions in American history.',
    3) RETURNING id INTO v_group_id;


    -- ── PASSAGE 4 [Natural Science]: "The Monarch Migration" ───
    VALUES (v_read_p2, 'Natural Science: "The Monarch Migration"',
'NATURAL SCIENCE: Adapted from "Navigating the Unknown" (© 2022).

Every autumn, hundreds of millions of monarch butterflies undertake one of the most remarkable migrations in the animal kingdom. From breeding grounds stretching across eastern North America — as far north as southern Canada — they converge on a single mountain range in the Mexican state of Michoacán, where they overwinter in dense clusters in the oyamel fir forests.

What makes the migration extraordinary is that no individual butterfly completes it more than once. The migrating generation — called the Methuselah generation for its unusual longevity of six to eight months, compared to the two to six weeks of summer generations — has never seen the overwintering site and will not survive to make the return journey. The round trip spans three to four generations, yet somehow the Methuselah butterflies navigate to the same trees their great-grandparents left.

Researchers have identified two primary navigational tools. First, monarchs use a sun compass, integrating the sun''s position with an internal circadian clock to maintain a consistent southwest heading. Second, evidence suggests they detect the Earth''s magnetic field, relying on this sense particularly when skies are overcast. A study by researchers at the University of Massachusetts found that disrupting monarchs'' magnetic sense significantly impaired their migratory heading.

The greatest threat to the monarch migration is habitat loss. Milkweed — the only plant on which monarchs lay eggs and the sole food source for caterpillars — has declined sharply across the American Midwest due to herbicide use. Combined with habitat destruction at the Mexican overwintering sites, these pressures have reduced the eastern monarch population by an estimated 80 percent since the 1990s.',
    4) RETURNING id INTO v_group_id;


    -- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
    -- PAPER 3 – READING PRACTICE TEST 3
    -- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

    -- ── PASSAGE 1 [Literary Narrative]: "Crossing" ─────────────
    VALUES (v_read_p3, 'Literary Narrative: "Crossing"',
'LITERARY NARRATIVE: Adapted from a memoir (© 2017).

My father arrived in New York on a Tuesday in April 1923. He was nineteen years old, and he carried a cardboard suitcase that his mother had packed with a change of clothes, a block of hard cheese wrapped in cloth, and a folded letter of introduction to a cousin he had never met. He spoke no English. The cousin''s address was written on a slip of paper he had sewn into the lining of his jacket.

He would tell the story often in later years, usually at the dinner table when the conversation ran slow, and he always began the same way: "The boat came into the harbor and I saw the statue and I thought it was a woman standing in the water." He''d pause there, waiting for the laugh, and then add: "I didn''t know yet it was made of copper."

What he rarely spoke of was the processing at Ellis Island — the long hours in the main hall, the inspector who mispronounced his name and wrote something else in the ledger, the moment when a man in a white coat looked into his eyes with a small instrument and he did not know if he would be allowed to stay. He told us about the harbor. He did not tell us about the fear.

I understand this now, at sixty-three, in a way I could not at twelve. He gave us the story he wanted us to have. The rest he carried differently.',
    1) RETURNING id INTO v_group_id;


    -- ── PASSAGE 2 [Social Science]: "The Architecture of Segregation" ──
    VALUES (v_read_p3, 'Social Science: "The Architecture of Segregation"',
'SOCIAL SCIENCE: Adapted from "How Policy Shaped American Cities" (© 2019).

In 1934, the federal government created the Federal Housing Administration (FHA) with the stated goal of making homeownership more accessible to Americans through government-backed mortgage insurance. The program transformed American housing — but not equally for all Americans.

The FHA developed a system of neighborhood risk assessments that assigned letter grades to urban areas. Neighborhoods deemed low risk — typically white, middle-class suburbs — received the highest ratings and qualified for federally backed loans. Neighborhoods with significant minority populations, particularly African American communities, were outlined in red on the maps — a practice that gave rise to the term "redlining." Banks and insurance companies adopted the same maps, effectively denying credit and insurance to residents of redlined areas regardless of their individual creditworthiness.

The consequences were profound and lasting. While white families took out FHA loans to purchase homes in expanding suburbs and build wealth through equity, African American families in redlined neighborhoods were locked out of that market. Unable to get mortgages, many were forced into predatory rent-to-own contracts. Without mortgage interest deductions or rising property values, they could not accumulate the same intergenerational wealth.

Researchers studying long-term economic outcomes have found that the boundaries of redlined neighborhoods in the 1930s still correlate strongly with lower median incomes, higher poverty rates, and lower homeownership rates today. A 2020 study found that historically redlined areas are on average 14 degrees Fahrenheit hotter than non-redlined areas in the same city, due to decades of underinvestment in tree cover and green space.',
    2) RETURNING id INTO v_group_id;


    -- ── PASSAGE 3 [Humanities]: "Gutenberg and the Revolution of Print" ──
    VALUES (v_read_p3, 'Humanities: "Gutenberg and the Revolution of Print"',
'HUMANITIES: Adapted from "The Press and the Public Mind" (© 2018).

When Johannes Gutenberg completed his printing press around 1440, he could not have predicted the world it would help create. The technology he developed — movable type cast from metal alloys, an oil-based ink that adhered to metal surfaces, and a screw press adapted from winemaking equipment — reduced the cost of producing a book by roughly 95 percent and increased production speed by several orders of magnitude. In the fifty years following Gutenberg''s first Bible, European printers produced an estimated twenty million books, more than all the scribes in Europe had produced in the previous thousand years combined.

The effects were not merely quantitative. Print standardized languages, creating shared written forms of German, French, and English that helped erode regional dialects and strengthen nascent national identities. It created new reading publics beyond the clergy and nobility — merchants, artisans, and eventually ordinary citizens who could access texts independently of the Church''s interpretive authority.

Perhaps the most dramatic consequence was the Protestant Reformation. Martin Luther''s Ninety-Five Theses, nailed to a church door in 1517, might have remained a local dispute had printers not reproduced them in their thousands within weeks. The ability to bypass the Church''s control of information transformed a theological argument into a continent-wide religious revolution.

Historians disagree about how much credit to give the printing press for the Reformation and the broader intellectual movements that followed. But most agree on this: Gutenberg''s invention did not merely speed up communication — it changed who had access to ideas, and therefore who could participate in shaping them.',
    3) RETURNING id INTO v_group_id;


    -- ── PASSAGE 4 [Natural Science]: "Life at the Abyss" ────────
    VALUES (v_read_p3, 'Natural Science: "Life at the Abyss"',
'NATURAL SCIENCE: Adapted from "Extreme Environments" (© 2021).

For most of the history of biology, it was assumed that sunlight was the foundation of all life on Earth. Photosynthesis — the process by which plants and algae convert light energy into sugars — was seen as the base of every food chain. This assumption was overturned in 1977, when scientists diving in the submersible Alvin discovered thriving ecosystems around hydrothermal vents on the floor of the Pacific Ocean, nearly 2,500 meters below the surface, far beyond the reach of any sunlight.

The vents — cracks in the seafloor through which superheated water rich in hydrogen sulfide and other chemicals is forced by geothermal activity — were surrounded by dense communities of organisms: tube worms up to two meters long, ghostly white crabs, mussels, and shrimp. The foundation of these ecosystems was not photosynthesis but chemosynthesis, the process by which certain bacteria convert chemical energy from hydrogen sulfide into organic matter. Every other organism in the community depended on these bacteria.

The discovery overturned long-held assumptions about life''s requirements. Scientists had believed that liquid water, sunlight, and a moderate temperature range were the three prerequisites for life as we know it. The hydrothermal vent communities eliminated sunlight from that list.

The implications extend beyond Earth. If life can thrive without sunlight in our own ocean, scientists reason, similar ecosystems might exist beneath the icy shells of Jupiter''s moon Europa and Saturn''s moon Enceladus, where subsurface oceans and hydrothermal vents may also exist. The deep abyss of our own planet has become a model for what life might look like elsewhere in the solar system.',
    4) RETURNING id INTO v_group_id;


    -- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
    -- PAPER 4 – READING PRACTICE TEST 4
    -- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

    -- ── PASSAGE 1 [Literary Narrative]: "The Cartographer's Daughter" ──
    VALUES (v_read_p4, 'Literary Narrative: "The Cartographer''s Daughter"',
'LITERARY NARRATIVE: Adapted from a novel (© 2020).

In the weeks after her father''s death, Mira began spending evenings in his study. She was not looking for anything in particular — or so she told herself. Mostly she sat in his chair, which still smelled faintly of the pipe tobacco he had given up twenty years ago, and let herself be surrounded by the maps.

There were hundreds of them. Some were framed on the walls; others were rolled and stacked in the corners or filed in flat wooden drawers he had built himself. They covered every continent and era: a hand-drawn rendering of the Silk Road from the fifteenth century, a nautical chart of the Caribbean from 1791, topographic surveys of the Cascade Mountains. Her father had been a geography professor for thirty years, and he had accumulated maps the way other people accumulate regrets.

It was in the third week that she found the unfinished ones. They were in a drawer she had not yet opened, stacked carefully, each protected by a sheet of tissue paper. They appeared to be maps of a single region she did not recognize — the coastline was unfamiliar, the topography unlike anything on any continent she knew. Some had notations in her father''s handwriting. One was dated three months before he died.

She held the last one for a long time. Her father had spent his life mapping real places. She had never imagined he might also have been mapping somewhere that existed only in his mind — or that she might one day be the one to finish it.',
    1) RETURNING id INTO v_group_id;


    -- ── PASSAGE 2 [Social Science]: "The Economics of Happiness" ──
    VALUES (v_read_p4, 'Social Science: "The Economics of Happiness"',
'SOCIAL SCIENCE: Adapted from "Wellbeing and Wealth" (© 2022).

In 1974, economist Richard Easterlin published a finding that became one of the most debated results in social science: while richer individuals within a country are generally happier than poorer ones, countries that grow wealthier over time do not seem to become correspondingly happier. This observation — known as the Easterlin Paradox — suggested that beyond a certain point, additional income does not improve overall life satisfaction.

The explanation most commonly offered is hedonic adaptation: the tendency of people to return fairly quickly to a stable baseline level of happiness after positive or negative life changes. A salary increase may boost happiness temporarily, but expectations adjust upward, and the person soon feels no more satisfied than before. The same mechanism applies to major purchases, career promotions, and even significant life events such as marriage.

More recent research has challenged the Easterlin Paradox. A 2021 study drawing on data from 1.7 million people across 164 countries found a consistent positive relationship between income and subjective wellbeing at all income levels — including in already-wealthy nations. The researchers argued that the earlier finding may have reflected the limitations of the data available in 1974.

Other economists argue the debate misses the point entirely. Studies consistently show that above middle-income levels, the relationship between income and happiness weakens sharply, and that non-economic factors — quality of social relationships, a sense of purpose, physical health, and autonomy at work — are the strongest predictors of sustained wellbeing. The conversation, these economists suggest, should be less about how much people earn and more about the conditions under which they live.',
    2) RETURNING id INTO v_group_id;


    -- ── PASSAGE 3 [Humanities]: "Voices of the Harlem Renaissance" ──
    VALUES (v_read_p4, 'Humanities: "Voices of the Harlem Renaissance"',
'HUMANITIES: Adapted from "The Making of a Cultural Movement" (© 2020).

In the 1920s, Harlem became the center of a cultural explosion now known as the Harlem Renaissance. Driven in part by the Great Migration that had brought more than a million African Americans from the rural South to northern cities, Harlem concentrated extraordinary artistic talent within a few square miles of upper Manhattan. Writers, musicians, visual artists, and intellectuals gathered in a neighborhood that offered, for a brief but luminous period, a sense of possibility rare in American life for Black citizens.

Langston Hughes, the movement''s most recognizable poet, articulated this spirit directly. His poems drew on the cadences of jazz and blues, insisting that African American vernacular language was not a literary deficiency but a source of power. "I, Too," published in 1926, declared the right of African American voices to occupy the center of American literary culture — with quiet, unshakable confidence.

Zora Neale Hurston brought a different but complementary approach. Trained as an anthropologist under Franz Boas at Columbia University, she traveled through the rural South collecting African American folklore, dialect, and oral tradition. Her 1937 novel Their Eyes Were Watching God wove these materials into a story of a Black woman''s search for identity — and was criticized by some contemporaries, including Hughes, for focusing on personal experience rather than political protest.

The disagreement between Hughes and Hurston reflects a broader tension within the Renaissance: between art as political weapon and art as cultural expression. Neither position prevailed, and the movement''s enduring achievement may lie precisely in this unresolved multiplicity of voices and purposes.',
    3) RETURNING id INTO v_group_id;


    -- ── PASSAGE 4 [Natural Science]: "The Moving Earth" ────────
    VALUES (v_read_p4, 'Natural Science: "The Moving Earth"',
'NATURAL SCIENCE: Adapted from "Earth in Motion" (© 2021).

In 1912, German meteorologist Alfred Wegener proposed that the continents had once formed a single landmass — which he called Pangaea — and had since slowly drifted apart. His evidence was suggestive: the coastlines of Africa and South America fit together like puzzle pieces, and identical fossil species appeared on continents separated by thousands of miles of ocean. But Wegener could not explain the mechanism by which continents, embedded in solid rock, could move through even harder oceanic rock, and for decades his theory was dismissed by most geologists.

Vindication came in the 1950s and 1960s, when oceanographers mapping the seafloor discovered a vast mountain range running down the center of the Atlantic Ocean. Studies of rocks on either side of this mid-ocean ridge revealed a striking pattern: rocks grew progressively older with distance from the ridge, suggesting that new seafloor was continuously being created at the ridge and spreading outward — carrying the continents with it. The mechanism Wegener lacked had been found.

The theory of plate tectonics — the modern synthesis of Wegener''s continental drift and seafloor spreading — holds that Earth''s outer layer is divided into roughly twenty rigid plates that move relative to one another. Where plates collide, mountains rise or one plate is forced beneath the other in a process called subduction, which can trigger earthquakes and volcanoes. Where plates pull apart, new crust is formed.

Recognition that Earth''s surface is in constant, slow motion has transformed geology into a dynamic, predictive science — one that explains why earthquakes cluster along plate boundaries and why the highest mountains on Earth were once the floor of an ancient sea.',
    4) RETURNING id INTO v_group_id;


END $$;
